**Required behaviour of `Promise.all(iterable)`**
- Returns a promise that resolves with an **array of results in input order** (not completion order), once every input has resolved.
- **Rejects immediately** with the first rejection reason.
- Inputs may be non-promise values; treat them as already resolved (`Promise.resolve(value)`).
- Empty input resolves with `[]`.

```js
function promiseAll(iterable) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const items = Array.from(iterable);
    const results = new Array(items.length);
    let pending = items.length;
    if (pending === 0) return resolve(results);
    items.forEach((item, i) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then(value => {
        results[i] = value;           // keep input order
        if (--pending === 0) resolve(results);
      }, reject);                      // first rejection wins; later calls are ignored
    });
  });
}
```
Points to explain: store by index so order is preserved; use a counter instead of `results.length` because holes may fill out of order; calling `resolve` or `reject` more than once is harmless since a promise settles only once.

**Promise polyfill outline.** A promise has state (pending, fulfilled, rejected), a value, and a queue of handlers. `then` returns a new promise; handlers run asynchronously (use `queueMicrotask` or `setTimeout`); resolving with a thenable must adopt its state; handler exceptions reject the derived promise. Mention the Promises/A+ spec as the reference.

**Related items**
- `useDebounce(value, delay)`: store the value in state, set a `setTimeout` in `useEffect` and clear it in the cleanup, so only the last change within `delay` ms is applied.
- `useThrottle`: allow at most one update per interval, tracking the timestamp in a `useRef`.
- Infinite scroll: an `IntersectionObserver` on a sentinel element triggers loading the next page; guard against duplicate fetches with a loading flag, stop at the last page, and clean up the observer.

